!!id:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/37/app/validate-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/37/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/37/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/37/io/ltr8/http/problem.tn"
@doc:"""
  The validator demo's own envelope: a schema and a document go in, the diagnostics come back.

  This is a CONFORMANCE tool as much as a demo. tson.io's home page runs the same two documents
  through the TypeScript implementation in the visitor's browser; this runs them through the Java
  one over HTTP, so the two verdicts can be put side by side. That is why the result carries the
  diagnostics whole rather than a summary: a code, a message and two source positions are what the
  implementations have to agree on.

  Both payloads are `text` and nothing here constrains them further, which is the point -- the
  service's whole job is to say whether they are well-formed, so a schema that rejected a malformed
  one at the envelope would answer the question by refusing to be asked it.

  It imports problem.tn for `diagnostic` rather than declaring its own. A diagnostic is a
  diagnostic whether it rides in an error body or in a result, and two shapes for one thing is how
  a client ends up with two renderers.
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    Which phase the diagnostics come from, and so what they are a verdict ON.

    The two run in order and the first to report stops the run, because a document cannot be checked
    against a schema that did not resolve. So SCHEMA means the schema is the thing at fault and the
    data was never looked at; DATA means the schema was sound and these are the document's own
    problems. Without this field a client cannot tell "your document is fine" from "your document
    was never checked", which is the same distinction the transport-level 5xx codes exist for.
    """
  validation_phase => !enum [SCHEMA DATA]

  @doc:"""
    What to check. `schema` absent asks for a Class 1 read -- base syntax and the built-in type
    vocabulary, no schema in scope -- which is a real mode rather than a degenerate one: it is what
    a JSON document gets, and what a document naming no !!schema gets.
    """
  validation_request => {
    schema?: text
    data:   text
  }

  @doc:"""
    Whether the document will be accepted by the processor that judged it -- the `tson` CLI's own
    outcome, so this service and `tson validate` can be put side by side. ACCEPTED: nothing was
    reported. REJECTED: the document is invalid, or this deployment refused it under its [TSON-DATA]
    §8.2 policy or §9.1 limits; one rejection settles it whatever else went unjudged. UNDETERMINED:
    nothing rejected it, but something could not be judged -- a schema not supplied, a construct this
    library has not implemented -- so acceptance cannot be stated. Which kind each problem is rides on
    its diagnostic's code.

    An enum and not a boolean, because `if (!conforming)` reads "undetermined" as "rejected".
    """
  outcome => !enum [ACCEPTED REJECTED UNDETERMINED]

  @doc:"""
    The answer. `outcome` restates `diagnostics` so a client can branch without reasoning about a
    list, and `elapsed_ms` times the validation alone -- not the request, and not the per-request
    bootstrap the service pays to keep one caller's schema out of another's.
    """
  validation_result => {
    outcome:     outcome
    phase:       validation_phase
    elapsed_ms:  float64
    diagnostics: [diagnostic]
  }
}
